WEBVTT

00:00.240 --> 00:01.073
Instructor: Okay, so now

00:01.073 --> 00:03.390
on to something a bit more interesting, hopefully.

00:03.390 --> 00:07.320
Every single reflog entry has a timestamp associated

00:07.320 --> 00:08.190
with it.

00:08.190 --> 00:09.780
If we take a look at one of them,

00:09.780 --> 00:12.750
here's the head reflog for this react repo.

00:12.750 --> 00:16.170
Every entry has a commit hash, right?

00:16.170 --> 00:18.415
It has the user who made it,

00:18.415 --> 00:21.570
but it also has a timestamp in here.

00:21.570 --> 00:23.027
So this is a timestamp,

00:23.027 --> 00:26.220
and this includes the date and the time

00:26.220 --> 00:29.010
that the head reference was updated.

00:29.010 --> 00:30.780
It's a Unix timestamp.

00:30.780 --> 00:33.090
Anyway, we can use this,

00:33.090 --> 00:35.970
or rather get can use it for us,

00:35.970 --> 00:38.250
if we pass through some

00:38.250 --> 00:41.580
of these nifty timed reference qualifiers.

00:41.580 --> 00:45.480
So instead of saying, "head at five moves ago",

00:45.480 --> 00:48.480
I can say "head at one day ago".

00:48.480 --> 00:51.810
Or "master branch two days ago" or "a week ago"

00:51.810 --> 00:52.643
or "yesterday".

00:52.643 --> 00:55.410
Or I can even provide a fully flushed out timestamp

00:55.410 --> 00:56.520
in different formats.

00:56.520 --> 00:58.800
If I want to know at an exact time,

00:58.800 --> 01:00.840
what did X branch look like?

01:00.840 --> 01:01.680
But remember,

01:01.680 --> 01:05.340
this does not go back more than typically 90 days.

01:05.340 --> 01:08.730
But also, it only includes local changes.

01:08.730 --> 01:10.953
So it doesn't matter what somebody else has done

01:10.953 --> 01:14.970
and what their head looked like over the last 90 days.

01:14.970 --> 01:18.330
It's just reflogs on your machine that are just a log

01:18.330 --> 01:20.940
of your local changes and movements.

01:20.940 --> 01:21.773
Okay?

01:21.773 --> 01:24.660
So I can use this syntax in place of-

01:24.660 --> 01:25.980
And it's the same syntax.

01:25.980 --> 01:27.570
What we've seen here were qualifiers,

01:27.570 --> 01:29.550
just a timed qualifier.

01:29.550 --> 01:32.130
And this definitely can be useful.

01:32.130 --> 01:33.030
Sometimes,

01:33.030 --> 01:35.183
if I wanna just compare what something looked like-

01:35.183 --> 01:37.860
Let's just take the master branch right now,

01:37.860 --> 01:38.760
today,

01:38.760 --> 01:41.220
compared to what it was yesterday.

01:41.220 --> 01:42.053
You know,

01:42.053 --> 01:43.620
I could go through the git log,

01:43.620 --> 01:45.030
and I could format it

01:45.030 --> 01:48.360
so that it also includes commit timestamps.

01:48.360 --> 01:49.260
And I could try and figure

01:49.260 --> 01:52.410
out which commit was two days ago.

01:52.410 --> 01:53.243
Or what?

01:53.243 --> 01:54.076
I said yesterday?

01:54.076 --> 01:55.350
Which commit I made yesterday,

01:55.350 --> 01:58.500
and then I could do a diff between now and that.

01:58.500 --> 02:02.790
Or I could just do "git diff between head",

02:02.790 --> 02:04.980
and then "head yesterday".

02:04.980 --> 02:06.573
So at yesterday.

02:07.741 --> 02:09.420
(keyboard clicking)

02:09.420 --> 02:11.370
And this is what's changed

02:11.370 --> 02:14.790
between now and yesterday on the master branch.

02:14.790 --> 02:15.720
Although actually,

02:15.720 --> 02:16.553
you know what?

02:16.553 --> 02:17.640
This is between head and head.

02:17.640 --> 02:19.800
That's probably not that useful.

02:19.800 --> 02:21.540
I would do this

02:21.540 --> 02:23.280
between the current master branch

02:23.280 --> 02:26.040
and the master branch tip yesterday.

02:26.040 --> 02:26.873
And there we go.

02:26.873 --> 02:28.680
Less has changed.

02:28.680 --> 02:30.840
Comparing between head and head yesterday,

02:30.840 --> 02:32.700
I mean, who knows what branch we're on.

02:32.700 --> 02:34.110
That's gonna impact it,

02:34.110 --> 02:35.160
and who knows what branch we were

02:35.160 --> 02:37.732
on yesterday at that point in time?

02:37.732 --> 02:42.060
So we can also do things like if I do "get ref log show",

02:42.060 --> 02:44.130
so I can filter what I look at

02:44.130 --> 02:47.730
in the ref log instead of "all entries".

02:47.730 --> 02:51.480
For head I can look at everything

02:51.480 --> 02:54.090
up until two days ago.

02:54.090 --> 02:56.793
So I can do two dot days ago,

02:57.870 --> 03:00.060
and it includes the time for me now.

03:00.060 --> 03:02.970
So it shows today's the 21st of February.

03:02.970 --> 03:04.413
Here's February 19th.

03:05.730 --> 03:07.770
So it also includes all the earlier stuff,

03:07.770 --> 03:09.423
up until two days ago.

03:10.830 --> 03:11.663
I also,

03:11.663 --> 03:13.920
I believe you can do two days,

03:13.920 --> 03:14.970
T W O.

03:14.970 --> 03:16.053
Or I know you can.

03:17.010 --> 03:18.393
So you can write that out.

03:18.393 --> 03:20.493
I can also do things like,

03:21.330 --> 03:22.620
I don't know, one hour ago.

03:22.620 --> 03:23.550
I don't think I have anything.

03:23.550 --> 03:25.413
How about one minute ago?

03:27.120 --> 03:28.050
Okay.

03:28.050 --> 03:29.160
So now we see-

03:29.160 --> 03:30.882
Oh, it's actually the 22nd, I guess.

03:30.882 --> 03:31.980
But anyway,

03:31.980 --> 03:34.260
we see that we have the time included here,

03:34.260 --> 03:35.610
and what time is it now?

03:35.610 --> 03:37.050
11 21.

03:37.050 --> 03:40.432
So this is the closest thing that it can go up to.

03:40.432 --> 03:43.380
And that's probably all I'll show here.

03:43.380 --> 03:44.527
We have these different options.

03:44.527 --> 03:45.514
"Yesterday",

03:45.514 --> 03:47.467
"multiple minutes ago",

03:47.467 --> 03:48.300
"hours ago",

03:48.300 --> 03:49.133
"days ago".

03:49.133 --> 03:50.100
You can do "weeks ago".

03:50.100 --> 03:51.990
I believe you can even do "months ago".

03:51.990 --> 03:56.990
I don't know if anything will show up for me one month ago.

03:57.990 --> 04:01.020
Yeah, it's just empty, but we don't get an invalid syntax.

04:01.020 --> 04:05.130
It tells us, "head ref log only goes back to this date".

04:05.130 --> 04:07.320
So this means if I wanted to go back and take a look

04:07.320 --> 04:09.690
at the master branch a week ago,

04:09.690 --> 04:11.040
when things were working,

04:11.040 --> 04:12.900
I know they were working a week ago,

04:12.900 --> 04:17.643
one option would just be "git checkout master" at,

04:18.810 --> 04:20.793
and then one dot week ago.

04:21.780 --> 04:23.550
So I don't have to find the commit hash.

04:23.550 --> 04:25.200
This is just gonna take me back

04:25.200 --> 04:28.290
to the closest matching reflog entry.

04:28.290 --> 04:29.550
Every entry in the reflog,

04:29.550 --> 04:31.440
every time that master tip changes,

04:31.440 --> 04:33.900
we get a new entry with a timestamp.

04:33.900 --> 04:36.780
And now, of course that puts me in detached head.

04:36.780 --> 04:39.180
And if we look at this git log-

04:39.180 --> 04:40.470
Here we are.

04:40.470 --> 04:42.930
Actually, why don't I just do regular git log?

04:42.930 --> 04:45.240
This was Friday, February 12th.

04:45.240 --> 04:46.800
So the closest thing that happened

04:46.800 --> 04:49.020
to that master tip a week ago.

04:49.020 --> 04:51.690
And here's that commit hash, right?

04:51.690 --> 04:54.750
But I am in detached head so I can get out of here,

04:54.750 --> 04:56.700
but that's one way we can use that.

04:56.700 --> 04:58.587
Now, what I'm gonna show you next,

04:58.587 --> 05:02.250
is how we can really use this stuff if we make mistakes,

05:02.250 --> 05:03.478
how we can undo

05:03.478 --> 05:07.260
or retrieve seemingly lost commits and information.

05:07.260 --> 05:08.820
And that's coming up next.

05:08.820 --> 05:09.653
But in this video,

05:09.653 --> 05:12.390
we saw that we can use this pattern

05:12.390 --> 05:13.740
to reference a particular entry

05:13.740 --> 05:14.760
in a ref log,

05:14.760 --> 05:16.770
but instead of just passing a number,

05:16.770 --> 05:18.240
like one or five,

05:18.240 --> 05:19.680
we can pass in these weird,

05:19.680 --> 05:20.790
but very nifty,

05:20.790 --> 05:21.623
timed references

05:21.623 --> 05:23.070
because every entry

05:23.070 --> 05:26.123
in the ref log also contains a timestamp associated with it.

05:26.123 --> 05:27.033
Okay.
